-- title:
--   Uniqueness of the Fricke automorphism of F_N^{full}
-- statement:
--   Fix a natural number $N \neq 0$ and let $F_N^{\mathrm{full}} =$ `modularFunctionFieldFull N` be the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$ generated by the set `divisorExpansions N` of all series $\mathrm{qExpand}_{\mathbb{Q}}\,d\,(jq)$ with $d \mid N$ and $d \neq 0$, where $\mathrm{qExpand}_{\mathbb{Q}}\,d$ is the ring homomorphism on Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$ induced by multiplying exponents by $d$ (substitution $q \mapsto q^{d}$) and $jq$ is the $q$-expansion of $j$; thus the generators are the expansions $j(q^{d})$. Let $\sigma$ be a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism of $F_N^{\mathrm{full}}$ satisfying `IsFrickeAutFull N σ`, i.e. for every pair of nonzero naturals $a, b$ with $ab = N$ one has $\sigma\bigl(j(q^{a})\bigr) = j(q^{b})$ inside $F_N^{\mathrm{full}}$. The conclusion is that $\sigma$ equals `frickeInvolutionFull N`, the total definition which is a choice of an automorphism satisfying `IsFrickeAutFull N` when one exists and the identity automorphism otherwise. In particular the hypothesis determines $\sigma$ uniquely, and under it the choice made in `frickeInvolutionFull N` is not an arbitrary one.
--
--   This is the uniqueness half of the construction of the Fricke (Atkin–Lehner) involution $w_N$ on the function field $\mathbb{Q}(j(q^{d}) : d \mid N)$ of the modular curve, characterised by its effect $j(q^{a}) \mapsto j(q^{b})$ on the generators for $ab = N$. It is used to identify the Atkin–Lehner involution attached to the trivial factorisation with `frickeInvolutionFull N`, and in the construction of models of modular curves from residue generators.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_IsFrickeAutFull_eq_frickeInvolutionFull.lean

